  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1250 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nA weak shortwave feature moving across the region will produce \nbrief high cloudiness over Eastern Idaho today. Temperatures \nremain above seasonal normals into early in the week, with high \ntemperatures reaching the mid-60s to low 70s across the lower \nelevations and Snake River Plain through Monday. Looking \nupstream on water vapor satellite imagery, a upper-level low \nnear 45N/140W will approach the Pacific Northwest coast before \ndropping southward just offshore through mid-week. Deep \nsoutherly flow will develop across the Great Basin into Eastern \nIdaho, gradually drawing moisture northward into the region. \nLight, isolated to widely scattered showers may begin as early \nas Tuesday afternoon and continue into Wednesday, though \nprecipitation during this initial phase will remain light and \nnon-impactful.\n\nThe upper low shifts inland across Central and Southern\nCalifornia Wednesday night into Thursday as a deep split trough\nbefore ejecting northeast through the Great Basin through\nFriday. Deeper moisture is anticipated late Thursday into\nFriday, bringing a more organized and widespread surge of\nprecipitation along with a sharp drop in temperatures. Exact\ntiming and accumulation totals remain variable as ensemble\nguidance shifts the track of the system; however, confidence\nremains high in a transition to a significantly cooler and\nwetter pattern by the end of the week. Expect daytime highs to\ndrop sharply by Friday, with snow levels lowering across higher\nterrain.",
